#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c5560ec9-32d6-514d-92fa-fbd77500d943" :question/math-academy-id "e-4431" :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ find $\\det(A)$." :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $A$ and $B$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det(AB) = \\det(A)\\cdot \\det(B)\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -\\sqrt{11} \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we notice that it's upper triangular. Therefore, the determinant is simply the product of the diagonal entries.\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $1$st column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & -\\sqrt{11} \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (1 \\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot (-2(-1)^{1 + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= (-9) \\cdot ((-2) \\cdot (-5 + 4)) \\\\\n&= (-9) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&=-18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "17487f8f-4974-5aaf-8729-6b1336ca5eaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4432" :question/problem "Find $\\mid A^{-1} \\mid$, if $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 14 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A^{-1} & = \\dfrac{1}{\\mid A\\end{vmatrix}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mid A \\mid$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & &= & 2 & 14\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\\\\n&= 7\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid \\\\\n&=-3(-1)^{3 + 2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (-12 + 7) \\\\\n&=-15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\mid A \\mid} = -\\dfrac{1}{15}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "d5d020cb-50ee-53f6-9d8b-d384bc9b7999" :question/math-academy-id "e-4433" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$\nfind\n$\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c + 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c + 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &= \\begin{vmatrix}a + 0 & b + 0 & c + 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + 0 & b + 0 & c + 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}_(⏟)_(-10) +\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-10 + 1(-1)^{1 + 3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-10 - 1 \\\\\n&=-11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adba491a-4709-5ca7-b820-a2f899939f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118648} {:answer/id #uuid "ae89bf6c-5a19-51c4-a9f6-6bc0772de6b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118649} {:answer/id #uuid "dc639648-0f70-5fba-97ee-b4df233f5842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118650} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc866d5-434c-5121-b817-39c9168e49b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118651} {:answer/id #uuid "7a39c6cd-1a0e-5267-9b55-9a49f5d7ab6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adba491a-4709-5ca7-b820-a2f899939f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118648} :answer-field/id #uuid "18fabfba-cbd9-5a73-9bc5-a1587c20225d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118647}] :question/id #uuid "b66a6d15-0b61-5b61-a152-b57545d60ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-42581" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$\\mid a + 3\\begin{vmatrix}-5 \\\\ b\\end{vmatrix}6|=-7$,\nfind\n$\\begin{vmatrix}a + 2 & -5 \\\\ b + 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8def6a81-a73b-5038-8dd8-26283be60ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref